Myth: In the valley of Nara, a circle of wells kept a promise: every act of kindness echoes back as a stronger current of life. The people tended a copper well of memory, its rim etched with names of those who helped and were helped. A blacksmith forged links of connection, a farmer shared seed, a nurse tended wounds without counting coins. Each gesture filled the well with brighter water, and every refill sent a note of gratitude fluttering through the village.

When a caravan trader arrived with glittering wares and a whisper of quick fortune, he urged the circle to send gifts only to him, promising to return them with interest. The wells rippled, then stilled; the current slowed, crops faltered, and the village grew wary. The trader’s gleam faded as the memory of generosity dimmed.

A child keeper of the well stepped forward, pouring out the last drop of fear and reciting a simple truth: true prosperity flows in the circle when trust is earned, not bought. The wells refilled, the air sang with revived kinship, and neighbors reconnected—sharing tools, time, and tenderness. The caravan departed, leaving behind a reminder that hearts repair a community faster than gold, and that trust, once restored, returns to every doorstep like rain.
